/// std/iter: the `Iterator` trait — the protocol a `for` loop consumes.
///
/// A type is an iterator when it can hand out its elements one at a time via
/// `next`: it yields `Some(item)` until exhausted, then `None` forever after.
/// `Item` is the element type, declared as an associated type so each iterator
/// fixes its own without a type parameter on the trait.
///
///     for x in it { ... }
///
/// desugars to a loop that calls `it.next()` and binds each `Some` payload to
/// `x`, stopping at the first `None`. The iterand must be a place (a variable,
/// field, index, or deref); bind a fresh-iterator expression to a `let` first.

import option { Option }

pub trait Iterator {
    type Item;
    proc next(*self) Option<Self::Item>;
}
